Date: Thu, 10 Jun 1993 11:19:38 -0500 (EST)
From: KMOSTA01@ULKYVX.LOUISVILLE.EDU
Subject: EC BOOKS: Fuzzy Logic

I have written a review of a book on fuzzy logic for "Contingencies", the
magazine of the American Academy of Actuaries. The book is fun to read
and I think it is EC, maybe even a desirable Extropian Reading. So I
am posting my review, but please do not copy or distribute without
permission, this is only a private communication -- the review will
appear in the next issue of "Contingencies".
Krzys'

Dr. Krzysztof Ostaszewski, C.F.A., A.S.A. Associate Professor of Mathematics, University of Louisville

Book Review: Fuzzy Logic: The Discovery of a Revolutionary Computer Technology -- and How It is Changing Our World, by Daniel McNeill and Paul Freiberger, Simon and Schuster, New York, 1993.

	Mathematics is a fascinating and powerful tool. And yet the great majority of people spend their entire lives without applying it, without even the slightest reference to any formal system of logic or scientific
precision. Such endeavors are generally left to highly skilled specialists, often branded as impractical theorists removed from every-day life,
nerds, engineers and actuaries. The nerds can have their revenge by earning high salaries, and holding publicUs incompetence in disdain. But the
likes of Star TrekUs Dr. McCoy always come back strongly by championing intuition, experience, and human insight. After all, actuaries do have talk
to their clients, and deal with rating agencies, and let us not have too high expectation of precision in those situations. Can there be a
reconciliation of the two worlds out there? 
Yes, there is an attempt to reconcile.
It is called fuzzy logic. It is often mistaken for an
intrusion of imprecision and vagueness into the kingdom of mathematics.
In fact, it is better described as an all-out invasion of the republic of
imprecision by the troops of the said kingdom disguised as local militia.
In other words, if you resolve to study fuzzy logic and expect to meet Dr
McCoy on your way there, you will be greatly disappointed, as you will
most likely  encounter Mr. Spock investigating intuition. 	A new book
entitled Fuzzy Logic: The Discovery of a Revolutionary Computer
Technology -- and How It Is Changing Our World  by Daniel McNeill and Paul
Freiberger, is a very well-researched and equally well-written exposition
of this new field of applied mathematics, which is quickly becoming a
technological phenomenon of the future. Fuzzy logic is based in the fuzzy
sets theory, originated in a paper published in 1965 by Lotfi A. Zadeh in
the journal Information and Control. The field has grown at an amazing
rate since then, and now there are more publications annually in fuzzy
sets than in actuarial science.What Lotfi Zadeh, a professor at the
University of California at Berkeley, proposed, is based on a strikingly
true recognition that uncertainty is not always related to lack of
information, easily resolved by a series of experiments providing such
information, but it could also be a result of immense complexity of a
problem, or its fundamental vagueness, both of which are often beyond
modeling capacity of the concepts of probability. When we set out to drive
a car, we do not know in advance all the possible outcomes of the drive,
and all possible scenarios of the driving process. Yet the set of rules we
have accumulated through learning and experience (the second one being
very important, as actuaries do know how young persons drive) provides us
with expertise necessary to perform the task. The rules are often
mprecise, vary from a person to 
person. But they do exist. Fuzzy logic
proposes to model them, and to eventually replace the human driver with a
computerized expert system using fuzzy logic.
The basic concept of the fuzzy sets theory is that of a fuzzy set. 
The classical set theory assumes that an element either belongs to a set,
or it does not. As in the classical Aristotelian logic,
a sentence is either true, or false. Zadeh proposed that there are sets,
whose elements belong to them with a certain degree
of membership. That degree is a number between 0 and 1,
with 0 representing no membership, and 1 representing complete certainty
of membership. With the help of that concept, he developed a theory of
approximate reasoning, a mathematical model for the often imprecise
rules which humans use in decision making, or controlling various
processes. The theory was met with very cold reception in the United
States, especially on the part of the National Science Foundation, but with
great enthusiasm in other countries, especially in Japan. Zadeh said
himself RI was fully cognisant that I was doing something that would
spark controversy".
Controversy did abound. Zadeh dealt with it patiently
in the United States. So there is controversy here, while in Japan firms
such as Matsushita, Sony, Sanyo, Sharp, Toshiba, and Canon already make
appliances, cameras, elevators, and other devices using fuzzy logic, and
the Ministry of International Trade and Industry has funded a major
research center, Laboratory for International Fuzzy Engineering Research.
McNeill and Freiberger provide an excellent introduction to the field
for a layperson. The book is written with great enthusiasm for the fuzzy
sets theory, which is a good selling point for the book and for the theory.
The authors carefully researched the lives and works of all scientists who
contributed to this lively area, even including Aristotle, whose classical
theory of logic has been a big stumbling block in general acceptance of
fuzzy logic

