Date: Thu, 10 Jun 93 18:18:58 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: QM: Many-Worlds

Here's the follow up I promised Stanton, on many-worlds.

Theories of quantum mechanics come in two parts, a physical part and a
philosophical part.

The physical part (the mathematical formalism, in the Max Jammer quote)
consists of the equations of physics, appropriate to the situation
(Schrodinger, Dirac or Klein-Gordon wave-equation or QED or electro-weak
or some GUT).  The equations involves mathematical objects or symbols
like the wavefunction, existing in abstract Hilbert spaces, but how to
interpret these objects is left open.  Which equations are correct is
the problem fundamental theoretical physics wrestles over.  Theorists
make predictions (guesses) which are confirmed or denied by experiment.

The philosophical part consists supplying an interpretation to the
symbols.  It might be thought that this would have to be done before the
equations could be guessed at or worked with, but in fact this is not
how the theories developed.  Schrodinger and Heisenberg constructed, and
published, their equivalent mathematical structures before they could
interpret them.  The interpretation later supplied by Born, Bohr,
Heisenberg and others, was that the square of modulus of the normalised
wavefunction corresponded to the probability density function.

The claim of many-worldists (including me) is that the interpretation
is implied by the standard equations (the formalism) alone.  And the
interpretation that results is the one describing worlds that split
whenever irreversible events take place - many-worlds.  All other
interpretations are false because they are mathematically or logically
incompatible with the standard equations or postulates.

Copenhagen is false because it postulates a dichotomy between conscious
observers and the rest of the universe, a distinction not reflected in
the standard laws of physics, and a return to primitive vitalism.

Bohm (in common with all hidden variablists) escapes from this by
looking for new equations, modifications of the standard equations, that
force a different interpretation.  I'm not intimately familiar with his
work, but I understand his (and others) work consists of slight
modifications of the Schrodinger equation, which have almost no effect
on the microscale, but which adds cumulatively to have macroscopic
effects.  He invoked non-relativistic potentials, but it is my
understanding that his successors have not succeeded in producing a
relativistic theory.

